registerRefData('nsw-cond-n-8429',{"id":8429,"pan":null,"ref":"DA2024/00345","address":"70 Henry Boultwood Drive FERNMOUNT NSW 2454","description":"Subdivision to create five (5) Community title lots inclusive of an association lot and ancillary works.","outcome":"approved","outcome_raw":"Approved (per determination document)","outcome_source":"tracker","determined":"2025-05-19","notice_url":"https://bellingen.greenlightopm.com/PublicDocument/download/570063004400720058007700670077006200740059003D00","sha256":"969df457499b3cc526dcf96f36a05214a98eb596e4375293448ab67427f1551c","pages":36,"layout":"numbered","n":6,"fetched_at":"2026-08-24T02:23:58+00:00","file":"n/8429.js","source_page":"https://bellingen.greenlightopm.com/Search/ApplicationLinkRedirect?trackingNumber=10240325hN4T4n2ko&isPublic=true","lga":"BELLINGEN","conditions":[{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"A","text":"Earth Water 13/02/2024\nARBORICULTURAL IMPACT AND TREE\nPROTECTION PLAN REPORT\n- Harbour Abor Undated\nSERVICING STRATEGY F Jed Civil 3 Feb 2025\nBUSHFIRE HAZARD ASSESSMENT FOR\nSUBDIVISION 70 HENRY BOULTWOOD\nDRIVE FERNMOUNT\nBFHA BJM Environmental 30/01/2025\nAny plans and / or documentation submitted to satisfy the Conditions of this consent.\nIn the event of any inconsistency between the approved plans and the supporting documentation,\nthe approved plans prevail. In the event of any inconsistency between the approved plans and a\ncondition of this consent, the condition prevails.\nNote: an inconsistency occurs between an approved plan and supporting documentation or\nbetween an approved plan and a condition when it is not possible to comply with both at the\nrelevant time.\nReason: To ensure all parties are aware of the approved plans and supporting documentation that\napplies to the development.\n2. Compliance with Other Department, Authority or Service Requirements\nDevelopment must be carried out in compliance with all recommendations and requirements,\nexcluding general advice, within the following:\nOther Department, Authority or Service NSW Planning\nPortal ref\nDated\nRural Fire Service - Rural Fires Act - S.100B - Ref:\nDA20250210000525-S38-1 - - Bush Fire Safety\nAuthority\nCNR-67893 30/4/2025\nNote: For a copy of the above referenced document/s, please refer to the NSW Planning Portal\nReason: To ensure the work is carried out in accordance with the determination and the statutory\nrequirements of other Department, Authority or Body.\n3. Signs on Site\nA sign must be erected in a prominent position on any site on which subdivision building work or\ndemolition work is being carried out:\nshowing the name, address, and telephone number of the principal certifier for the work, and\nshowing the name of the principal contractor (if any) for any building work and a telephone\nnumber on which that person may be contacted outside working hours, and\nstating that unauthorised entry to the work site is prohibited.\nAny such sign is to be maintained while the subdivision work or demolition work is being carried\nout but must be removed when the work has been completed.\nNote: This does not apply in relation to building work or demolition work that is carried out inside\nan existing building that does not affect the external walls of the building.\nReason: Prescribed condition EP&A Regulation, clauses 70 (2) and (3).\n4. Addressing\nThe addressing for the development is below\n• Proposed Lot 81 - 66 Henry Boultwood Drive, Fernmount NSW 2454\n• Proposed Lot 82 - 70 Henry Boultwood Drive, Fernmount NSW 2454\n• Proposed Lot 83 - 68 Henry Boultwood Drive, Fernmount NSW 2454\n• Proposed Lot 84 - 625 Waterfall Way, Fernmount NSW 2454\n• Proposed Lot 85 - 619 Waterfall Way, Fernmount NSW 2454\nReason: to ensure the development is appropriately addressed.\nSUBDIVISION WORK\nBEFORE ISSUE OF A SUBDIVISION WORKS CERTIFICATE\n5. Subdivision Works Certificate\nSubdivision works the subject of this development consent MUST NOT be commenced\nuntil:\n(a) A Subdivision Works Certificate for the subdivision work has been issued by\nCouncil, and\n(b) The person having the benefit of the development consent has given Council\nwritten notice, at least two days prior to work commencing on site, the date\nsubdivision work is proposed to commence.\nReason: To comply with the Environmental Planning & Assessment Act 1979.\n6. Long Service Levy\nBefore the issue of a subdivision works certificate , the long service levy, as calculated at\nthe date of this consent, must be paid to the Long Service Corporation under the Building\nand Construction industry Long Service Payments Act 1986, section 34, and evidence of\nthe payment is to be provided to the Certifier.\nReason: To ensure the long service levy is paid.\n7. Roads Act Approval\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate a separate consent pursuant to the\nRoad Act 1993 shall be issued for works required within the road reserve.\nReason: To ensure the legality of the development.\n8. Traffic Control Plan\nBefore the issue of a subdivision works certificate, consent from Council must be obtained\nfor a traffic management plan pursuant to Section 138 of the Roads Act 1993.\nThe plans and specifications are to include the measures to be employed to control traffic\n(inclusive of construction vehicles) during construction of the development.\nThe traffic control plan is to be designed in accordance with the requirements of the Roads\nand Traffic Authority’s Manual, Traffic Control at Work Sites , and Australian Standard\n1742.3 - 1985, Manual of Uniform Traffic Control Devices Part 3, ‘Traffic Control Devices\nfor Works on Roads’.\nThe plan must incorporate measures to ensure that motorists using the road adjacent to\nthe development and residents and pedestrians in the vicinity are subjected to minimal\ntime delays due to the approved works.\nThe traffic control plan must be prepared by a suitably qualified and TfNSW accredited\nWork Site Traffic Controller.\nReason: To minimise safety risks and impacts on traffic flows during works.\n9. Engineering Construction Plans\nEngineering construction plans and specifications must accompany the Subdivision\nWorks Certificate application. Such plans are to provide for the works in the following\nlist in accordance with Council’s current Design and Construction Manuals and\nSpecifications.\n• Kerb & Gutter, Road Shoulder Construction - Kerb and gutter, road shoulder and\nassociated drainage construction, footpath formation and turfing including any\nnecessary relocation of services across the frontage of the subdivision.\n• Gutter Crossings - Gutter crossings to each of the proposed new allotments, where\nother than a rollover kerb is used.\n• Driveways Rural - sealed roads - Sealed driveways, in accordance with Council’s\nrural driveway standards, from the edge of the bitumen to 3 metres within the\nproperty boundary to each of the proposed new allotments.\n• Access Handle - A five (5) metre wide reinforced concrete driveway (or equivalent),\ntogether with all necessary services/service conduits (water, telephone, power,\nstormwater) within the access handle of proposed Lot 1.\n• Service Conduits - Service conduits to each of the proposed new allotments laid in\nstrict accordance with the service authorities’ requirements.\n• Stripping and Stockpiling - Stripping and stockpiling of existing topsoil on site, prior\nto commencement of earthworks, and the subsequent re-spreading of this material\ntogether with a sufficient quantity of imported topsoil so as to provide a minimum\nthickness of 80mm over the allotments and footpaths and public reserves, upon\ncompletion of the development works.\n• Stormwater Outlets - An energy dissipating pit with a suitably installed locked\ngrated outlet to all pipes or any other drainage structures. Grates must be of\ngalvanised weldlock construction.\n• Stormwater Quality - Stormwater quality must be suitable for discharge in\naccordance with Department of Land and Water Conservation NSW (1998) The\nConstructed Wetlands Manual and Landcom (March 2004), Managing Urban\nStormwater, Soils and Construction.\n• Access to Stormwater Structures - An all-weather gravel vehicle access must be\nprovided to detention ponds, drainage discharge points, filter controls and wetland\nareas for access by The Community for maintenance purposes.\n• Filling to above Flood Level - Filling of Lots 4 & 5 with clean suitable material to 6.9\nm AHD being a level equivalent to the 1% flood level. Note: The General Flood\nPlanning Level (GFPL) of the site, being the 1% AEP + 0.5m freeboard, is 7.4m AHD.\n• Tree disposal - Details of the means of tree disposal are to be submitted for\nCouncil’s approval prior to the commencement of subdivision works.\nReason: To ensure appropriate detail is provided for works required to be undertaken as\npart of the development.\n10. Stormwater Management Plan\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate, detailed site -based stormwater\nmanagement plan and computer modelling demonstrating compliance with Level 3\ntreatment requirements as per Chapter 12 of Council’s DCP 2017 shall be submitted an\nendorsed by Council. The site-based management plan shall:\nGenerally, be in accordance with Bellingen Shire Council Water Sensitive Urban Design\nGuidelines.\nIndicate that the bioretention basins shall act as a sediment basin during the build out\nphase and bioretention maintenance period.\nIndicate once the contributing catchment achieves eighty percent (80%) build-out\nundertake the following works:","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":4},{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"Convert the basin from a sediment basin to a bioretention basin,","text":"","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":8},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"Undertake in-situ hydraulic conductivity testing of filter material; and","text":"","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":8},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"If the testing demonstrates that the filter media achieves the design hydraulic","text":"conductivity, plant out the basin in accordance with the approved drawings.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":8},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"Indicate should the bioretention maintenance period expire and less than 80%","text":"of the contributing catchment is fully developed payment to the value the\napproved bond to cover the cost of the conversion of the basin will be donated\nto Council.","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":8},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"","text":"Be certified by a suitably qualified and practicing engineer.\nFurther analysis of the hydrology and hydraulics of the wider catchment to be undertaken\nto confirm the overflow from the basin will not be more than the existing flow down the\ngully. Additionally, a flood study of the unnamed tributary is to be provided to determine\n1% AEP flood levels.\nReason: To ensure that stormwater treatments are designed to meet the needs of the\nreceiving environment.\n11. Flood Assessment Report\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate, a detailed flood assessment report\nin accordance with Appendix 8.2 of Development Control Plan 2017 shall be submitted\nand endorsed by Council.\nThe report shall demonstrate that compensatory earthworks will have no adverse\nmeasurable impacts beyond the property boundary on flood levels over the full range of\nfloods.\nThe report shall be certified by a suitably qualified and practicing engineer.\nReason: To ensure that stormwater treatments are designed to meet the needs of the\nreceiving environment\n12. Section 68 Approval - Water mains\nWater mains are to be extended to service all lots. This consent includes an approval\nunder Section 68 of the Local Government Act 1993 to connect all lots to Council’s\ninfrastructure for water.\nEngineering plans of the proposed water and stormwater works shall be submitted for\nCouncil’s approval prior to the issue of the Subdivision Works Certificate.\nPayment of a fee in accordance with Councils Fees and Charges for the installation of a\nwater meter to each lot must be paid prior to the issue of the Subdivision Works\nCertificate.\nReason: To ensure appropriate services are provided to each lot.\n13. Electricity Infrastructure\nSince electricity infrastructure is located within the property and/or within close\nproximity of the property, any activities approved by this consent within these locations\nmust be undertaken in accordance with the latest industry guideline currently known as\nISSC 20 Guideline for the Management of Activities within Electricity Easements and\nClose to Infrastructure.\nApproval may be required from Essential Energy should activities within the property\nencroach on the electricity infrastructure.\nReason: To minimise safety risks associated with working in proximity to power.\n14. Utilities and services\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision works certificate, written evidence of the following\nservice provider requirements must be provided to Bellingen Shire Council:\na. a letter from Essential Energy demonstrating that satisfactory arrangements\ncan be made for the installation and supply of electricity; and\nb. a response from Bellingen Shire Council - Water and Wastewater Division as\nto whether the plans accompanying the application for a subdivision works\ncertificate would affect any Bellingen Shire Council - Water and Waste Water\nDivision infrastructure, and w hether further requirements need to be met;\nand\nc. other relevant utilities or services - that the development as proposed to be\ncarried out is satisfactory to those other service providers, or if it is not, the\nchanges that are required to make the development satisfactory to them.\nReason: To ensure relevant utility and service providers’ requirements are provided to the\ncertifier.\n15. Landscaping\nA detailed landscaping plan prepared by an appropriately qualified person in accordance\nwith the requirements of clauses 9.6.1 and 9.6.4 of the Bellingen Shire Development\nControl Plan 2010 shall be submitted to and endorsed by Council prior to the issue of a\nSubdivision Works certificate. The landscape plan shall include a landscape maintenance\nschedule.\nReason: To preserve and encourage landscaped settings.\n16. Street trees - Community title subdivision planting scheme\nA community title subdivision street tree planting scheme prepared in accordance with\nthe requirements of Council’s Street tree guidelines contained within Bellingen Shire\nDevelopment Control Plan 2017 must be submitted to and approved by Council prior to\nthe issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate.\nThe plan must show all services. Plants to be used in the street tree planting scheme must:\n• Be a minimum size of 2 metres in height,\n• Have a 50mm calliper diameter,\n• Be sourced from at least a 25-litre container,\n• Be protected by a suitable tree guard (e.g. a 750mm square tree guard made from\na 3m “Acacia” panel painted “Caulfield Green”),\n• Be suitably supported by two 2100mm timber picket posts,\n• Provided with slow-release fertiliser,\n• Include the provision of 125mm flexible agricultural pipe filled with 14mm blue\nmetal for watering of the plant, and\n• Be mulched with 100mm of native tree mulch at least 1.5m from the base of the\ntree or to any impervious area (whichever is closer).\nReason: To improve the amenity of the streetscape.\n17. Compensatory Planting Plan\nA detailed Compensatory Planting Plan must be prepared submitted to Council and\napproved by Council prior to the issue of a Subdivision Works Certificate.\nThe plan must address the compensatory planting requirements resulting from the\napproved removal of two (2) Primary Koala Food Trees (Tree 8 and Tree 10), as\nidentified in the Arboricultural Impact and Tree Protection Plan Report prepared by Ross\nLeaver (dated 14 September 2024).\nThe Compensatory Planting Plan must:\na) Be prepared by a qualified ecologist, bush regenerator, or arborist with demonstrated\nexperience in koala habitat restoration.\nb) Identify the location(s) for planting twenty (20) juvenile Koala Food Trees at a\nminimum ratio of 10:1, in accordance with Chapter 16 of the Bellingen Shire DCP 2017 -\nKoala Habitat Protection.\nc) Ensure all planting occurs:\n• Within or adjacent to the mapped Secondary “A” Koala Habitat Area on the site;\n• In areas ecologically suitable for koala habitat restoration;\n• At least 5 metres from any existing large tree trunks or road pavement;\n• Outside the Asset Protection Zone (APZ), effluent disposal areas, and other\ncleared infrastructure zones.\nd) Specify the planting of the following species (or other suitable locally indigenous\nkoala food trees as agreed to in writing by Council):\n• Eucalyptus microcorys (Tallowwood) - 10\n• Eucalyptus propinqua - 5\n• Eucalyptus saligna - 5\ne) Include the following implementation and protection measures:\n• Use of locally sourced provenance stock;\n• Installation of protective guards (minimum 1.5m high);\n• Mulching (50-100mm depth, minimum 300mm radius);\n• Initial and ongoing watering schedule for establishment (e.g., 3x weekly for one\nmonth, then weekly for 6 months);\n• Maintenance schedule for weed control and guard removal once trees are\nestablished.\nf) Include a site map identifying planting locations (to scale), species layout, and\nseparation distances.\ng) Be accompanied by a brief supporting statement confirming the plan’s compliance\nwith the above requirements and DCP guidelines.\nThe approved Compensatory Planting Plan must be fully implemented by the proponent\nprior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate, or in accordance with a staged\nimplementation schedule agreed in writing by Council.\nReason: To ensure the trees to be planted are design and approved.\n18. Construction Site Management Plan\nBefore the issue of a subdivision works certificate, a construction site management plan\nmust be prepared, and provided to the satisfaction of the certifier.\nThe plan must include the following matters:\na) The location and materials for protective fencing and hoardings on the perimeter\nof the site.\nb) Provisions for public safety.\nc) Pedestrian and vehicular site access points and construction activity zones.\nd) Details of construction traffic management including:\ni. Proposed truck movements to and from the site.\nii. Estimated frequency of truck movements; and\niii. Measures to ensure pedestrian safety near the site.\ne) Details of bulk earthworks to be carried out.\nf) The location of site storage areas and sheds.\ng) The equipment used to carry out works.\nh) The location of a garbage container with a tight-fitting lid.\ni) Dust, noise and vibration control measures.\nj) The location of temporary toilets.\nk) The protective measures for the preservation of trees on -site and in adjoining\npublic areas including measures in accordance with:\ni. AS 4970 - Protection of trees on development sites.\nii. Bellingen Shire Development Control Plan 2017.\niii. An arborist’s report approved as part of this consent.\nA copy of the construction site management plan must be kept on -site at all times while\nwork is being carried out.\nReason: To require details of measures that will protect the public, and the surrounding\nenvironment, during site works and construction.\n19. Erosion and Sediment Control Plan\nBefore the issue of a subdivision works certificate, an erosion and sediment control plan\nmust be prepared by a suitably qualified person in accordance with the following\ndocuments and provided to the Certifier:\na) Council’s Development Control Plan,\nb) The guidelines set out in the NSW Department of Housing manual ‘Managing\nUrban Stormwater: Soils and Construction Certificate’ (the Blue Book) (as\namended from time to time), and\nc) The ‘Do it Right On -Site, Soil and Water Management for the Construction\nIndustry' (Southern Sydney Regional Organisation of Councils and the Natural\nHeritage Trust) (as amended from time to time).\nReason: To ensure no substance other than rainwater enters the stormwater system and\nwaterways.\nBEFORE SUBDIVISION WORK COMMENCES\n20. Subdivision Works Certificate\nSubdivision works the subject of this development consent MUST NOT be commenced\nuntil:\n(c) A Subdivision Works Certificate for the subdivision work has been issued by\nCouncil, and\n(d) The person having the benefit of the development consent has given Council\nwritten notice, at least two days prior to work commencing on site, the date\nsubdivision work is proposed to commence.\nReason: To comply with the Environmental Planning & Assessment Act 1979.\n21. Public Liability Insurance\nBefore subdivision works commence, the developer and/or contractor must produce\nevidence to the Principal Certifier of public liability insurance cover for a minimum of $20\nmillion.\nCouncil is to be nominated as an interested party on the policy.\nThe public liability insurance cover is to be maintained for the duration of the period of\nthe works and during any maintenance period.\nReason: To protect public health and safety.\n22. Erosion & sediment measures\nBefore subdivision works commence, erosion and sedimentation controls are to be in\nplace in accordance with the approved Erosion and Sediment Control Plan.\nNote: Council may impose on-the-spot fines for non-compliance with this condition.\nReason: To ensure erosion controls are in place before works commence.\n23. Erosion & sediment measures\nErosion and sedimentation controls are to be in place in accordance with Managing Urban\nStormwater - Soils and Construction Vol 1, 4th Edition prepared by Landcom and\nDevelopment Control Plan (Erosion and Sediment Control) 2009.\nNote: Council may impose on-the-spot fines for non-compliance with this condition.\nReason: To ensure erosion controls are in place before works commence.\n24. Toilet facilities\nToilet facilities must be available at or in the vicinity of the work site before works begin\nat a ratio of one toilet plus one additional toilet for every twenty (20) persons employed\nat the site. Each toilet must be:\na) A standard flushing toilet connected to a public sewer; or\nb) Connected to an on -site effluent disposal system approved under the Local\nGovernment Act 1993; or\nc) Be a portable hire toilet which is regularly serviced by the hire agent. All waste\nmust be disposed of at a lawful premises.\nThe availability of each toilet must be maintained until the works are completed.\nReason: To provide adequate facilities on site for workers and mitigate amenity impacts.\n25. Imported Fill\nPrior to the location of any fill on the site, suitable evidence is to be provided to the\nsatisfaction of Council demonstrating that all fill imported to the site is virgin excavated\nnatural material (VENM) as defined by the Protection of the Environment Operations Act\n1997. This may require the material to be sampled and analysed for potential\ncontaminants by a NATA accredited laboratory.\nReason: To ensure only clean fill is brought onto the property.\n26. Environmental Management Plan\nPrior to the commencement of subdivision works, a comprehensive Environmental\nManagement Plan (EMP) must be submitted to and approved by Council.\nThe EMP must include the following components as a minimum:\na) An erosion and sediment control plan designed in accordance with the\nrequirements of the Landcom (March 2004), Managing Urban Stormwater,\nSoils and Construction which addresses:\ni. General site management, material handling practices, soil\nstabilisation, water control, sediment control, wind erosion control\nand access measures.\nii. Installation of erosion and sediment control measures prior to\nconstruction.\niii. Regular inspection of erosion and sediment control measures,\nparticularly following rainfall events, to ensure their ongoing\nfunctionality.\niv. Restriction of stockpiles to identified construction compounds, in\nareas of cleared land and exotic grassland and management of\nthese stockpiles to ensure no offsite impacts through dust\ngeneration or sedimentation.\nv. Areas of bare ground to be stabilised as soon as practicable after\nconstruction to minimise the time bare earth is exposed to erosion\nand weed invasion.\nb) A vegetation management plan, including:\ni. An accurate to scale tree plan showing the trees to be removed and\nthose to be retained as part of the development.\nTrees to be retained are to be clearly identified on site by distinctive temporary taping.\nOnly the minimum extent of clearing necessary to create any APZ will be permitted. In\nthis regard, at least 14% of the tree canopy cover within the inner protection area and\n29% in the outer protection area are to be retained.\nii. Communication with construction personnel of the conservation\nvalue of surrounding habitats and their responsibilities with regards\nto protection of these habitats during construction.\nc) A weed management plan, including a description of:\ni. Type and location of weeds of concern (including noxious weeds)\nwithin the subject site.\nii. Sensitive receivers (such as native vegetation, drainage lines and\nwaterways) within or adjacent to the subject site.\niii. Measures to prevent the spread of weeds, including hygiene\nprocedures for equipment, footwear and clothing.\niv. Proposed weed control methods and targeted areas.\nv. Weed disposal protocols.\nd) A fauna management plan, including, but not limited to the following:\ni. A fauna management protocol, including pre-clearing surveys for\nnesting or roosting terrestrial fauna.\nii. A habitat feature protocol, including pre-clearing surveys for habitat\nfeatures such as hollow logs and hollow-bearing trees that can be\nretained or salvaged and placed in adjoining retained vegetation,\nand protocols for the safe clearing of hollow-bearing trees to ensure\nno resident fauna are injured.\niii. Careful removal and relocation of hollow resources cleared form the\nsubject site into proximate areas.\ne) A pre-clearing protocol which should include, but not be limited to:\ni. Clear marking/erection of exclusion fencing around protected\nvegetation areas and delineation of 'no go' areas.\nii. Inspections of native vegetation for other resident fauna and/or\nnests or other signs of fauna occupancy.\niii. Capture and relocation or captive rearing of less mobile fauna (such\nas roosting microbats, nestling birds or any injured fauna) by a\ntrained and experienced wildlife handler and with assistance from\nWIRES (or similar).\niv. Inspection and identification/marking of hollow-bearing trees\nadjacent to construction footprints to help ensure against accidental\nimpacts.\nv. Pre-clearing surveys are to be undertaken by suitably qualified and\nexperienced ecologists.\nf) An A4 handout must be prepared which provides illustrations of Aboriginal\nartefacts that might be found on the subject land, a written procedure to be\nfollowed in the event that artefacts are found, and a brief warning as to the\nlikely outcome if the procedure is ignored. The handout must be provided to\nall operators of earthmoving equipment within the subject land.\nReason: To minimise the environmental impact of the development.\nDURING SUBDIVISION WORK\n27. Earthworks\nAll earthworks shall be undertaken in accordance with AS 3798 -2007 Guidelines on\nearthworks for commercial and residential developments and must not cause:\n(a) a danger to life or property; or\n(b) damage to any adjoining building or structure on the lot or to any building or\nstructure on any adjoining lot.\nReason: To ensure that earthworks meet current standards and provide stable landforms.\n28. Earthworks (Final Landforms)\nThe surface levels and profiles of all infrastructure appurtenances, including electrical and\ntelecommunications pits or covers must be finished consistent with the designed and\nconstructed surface levels and planes of the finished development. These installations\nmust not create trip hazards or discontinuities with the finished land profiles.\nReason: To minimise the likely impacts of the development in respect of safety,\nconstruction, amenity, site attributes and public interest.\n29. Earthworks (Compaction Testing)\nThe earthworks shall be tested for compaction and stability at a National Association of\nTesting Authorities accredited soil testing laboratory and assessed by a qualified\ngeotechnical engineer to confirm that the works have achieved typical standards for\nresidential development in accordance with AS 2870-2011 Residential slabs and footings\nand AS 3798 -2007 Guide lines on earthworks for commercial and residential\ndevelopments. If the compaction requirements for the fill have not been satisfied, then\nthe fill material shall be lifted and re -laid and re -compacted to achieve the typical\nstandards. All test results and geotechnical certification shall be submitted to Council for\nrecord purposes upon the practical completion of earthworks.\nReason: To ensure that fill is suitable for residential development.\n30. Earthworks (imported fill)\nThe only fill material that may be received at the development site is:\n(a) virgin excavated natural material (VENM) within the meaning of Part 3 of\nSchedule 1 to the Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997;\n(b) any other waste-derived material the subject of a resource recovery exemption\nunder clause 51A of the Protection of the Environment Operations (Waste)\nRegulation 2014 that is permitted to be used as fill material, excluding waste tyre;\nor\n(c) other material approved in writing by the NSW Environment Protection Authority.\nAny fill material received at the development site must be accompanied by accurate\nrecords detailing the volume and type of material received. The records must be retained\nby the proponent and made available to the principal certifying authority on request.\nReason: To ensure that fill is devoid of contaminants that could harm or potentially harm\nthe receiving environment and users of the land.\n31. Earthworks (Exported Cut)\nAll excavated material to leave the site shall be disposed of at an approved landfill facility.\nAlternatively, where it is proposed to dispose of the excavated material at another\nlocation, no material shall leave the site until:\n(a) Council has been advised in writing of the destination site(s); and\n(b) Council has been advised of the quantity and makeup of the material; and\n(c) Council has issued written approval for disposal to the alternate location(s).\nNote 1 : The exportation of fill or soil from the site must be in accordance with the\nprovisions of the Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997 and the Office of\nEnvironment and Heritage ‘Waste Classification Guidelines’ and shall comply with the\nterms of any approval issued by Council.\nNote 2: Development consent may be required under the Environmental Planning and\nAssessment Act 1979 for the receiving land.\nReason: To ensure that excavated material is lawfully disposed.\n32. Imported Fill\nPrior to the location of any fill on the site, suitable evidence is to be provided to the\nsatisfaction of Council demonstrating that all fill imported to the site is virgin excavated\nnatural material (VENM) as defined by the Protection of the Environment Operations Act\n1997. This may require the material to be sampled and analysed for potential\ncontaminants by a NATA accredited laboratory.\nReason: To ensure only clean fill is brought onto the property.\n33. Tree Removal\nThe following trees, identified in the Arboricultural Impact and Tree Protection Plan\nReport prepared by Ross Leaver (Harbour Arbor, dated 14 September 2024), are\napproved for removal as part of this development:\nA. Trees to be removed due to structural risk (QTRA risk assessment):\n• Tree 8 - Eucalyptus microcorys (Tallowwood)\n• Tree 9 - Eucalyptus siderophloiea (Ironbark)\n• Tree 10 - Eucalyptus microcorys (Tallowwood)\nB. Trees to be removed due to direct conflict with the proposed development footprint\n(access road construction):\n• Tree 11 - Callistemon citrinus (Bottlebrush)\n• Tree 12 - Archirhodomyrtus beckleri (Rose Myrtle)\n• Tree 13 - Grevillea robusta (Silky Oak)\n• Tree 14 - Callistemon citrinus (Bottlebrush)\n• Tree 15 - Melaleuca leucadendra (Weeping Paperbark)\n• Tree 16 - Leptospermum patersonii (Lemon-scented Tea Tree)\n• Tree 19 - Eucalyptus microcorys (Tallowwood)\nNo other vegetation is to be removed without prior written approval from Council. All\nworks must be undertaken in accordance with relevant conditions of this consent, the\nTree Protection Plan, and AS 4970-2009 Protection of Trees on Development Sites.\nNo trees shall be removed prior to the issue of Subdivision Works Certificate.\nReason: To ensure the trees approved are removed.\n34. Sediment and Erosion Control\nSediment and soil erosion control measures must be implemented, managed and\nmaintained during all development works to prevent sediment leaving the site or entering\ndownstream properties, roads, drainage systems, waterways or environmentally sensitive\nareas. Sediment and soil erosion control measures must be designed and implemented\nin accordance with the Landcom publication Managing Urban Stormwater: Soils and\nConstruction, Volume 1, 4th Edition (reprinted July 2006) and any approved plans to the\nsatisfaction of the principal certifying authority.\nReason: To minimise and control erosion and sedimentation.\n35. Works in Accordance with Council standards\nAll works must comply with:\na) Conditions of this consent\nb) Council endorsed design drawings\nc) Council endorsed Certified Stormwater Management Plan\nd) Water Services Association of Australia Sewerage Codes\ne) Bellingen Shire Council Standard Conditions for Engineering Works Associated\nwith Developments\nf) Development Control Plan 2017\ng) Contemporary industry standards\nReason: To ensure work associated with the development is carried out to an acceptable\nstandard.\n36. Construction Times\nAny subdivision works involving the generation of noise which extends beyond the\nboundary of the land, other than works required in an emergency to avoid the loss of life,\ndamage to property and/or to prevent environmental harm, shall only be carried out\nbetween 7:00 am and 6:00 pm Monday to Saturday inclusive. No work shall occur on\nSundays or public holidays.\nThe builder/site manager is responsible to instruct and control sub-contractors regarding\nthe hours of work.\nReason: To minimise amenity impacts on occupants of surrounding properties.\n37. Implementation of the Site Management Plans\nWhile subdivision site work is being carried out:\na) the measures required by the construction site management plan and the erosion\nand sediment control plan (plans) must be implemented at all times, and\nb) a copy of these plans must be kept on site at all times and made available to\ncouncil officers upon request.\nReason: To ensure site management measures are implemented during the carrying out\nof site work.\n38. Soil Management\nWhile subdivision site work is being carried out, the principal certifier must be satisfied all\nsoil removed from or imported to the site is managed in accordance with the following\nrequirements:\na) All excavated material removed from the site must be classified in accordance\nwith the EPA’s Waste Classification Guidelines before it is disposed of at an\napproved waste management facility and the classification, and the volume of\nmaterial removed must be reported to the principal certifier.\nb) All fill material imported to the site must be:\ni. Virgin Excavated Natural Material as defined in Schedule 1 of the Protection\nof the Environment Operations Act 1997, or\nii. a material identified as being subject to a resource recovery exemption by the\nNSW EPA, or\niii. a combination of Virgin Excavated Natural Material as defined in Schedule 1\nof the Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997 and a material\nidentified as being subject to a resource recovery exemption by the NSW EPA.\nReason: To ensure soil removed from the site is appropriately disposed of and soil\nimported to the site is not contaminated and is safe for future occupants.\n39. Maintenance of Erosion and Sediment Control Measures\nErosion and sediment control measures must be maintained at all times until the site has\nbeen stabilised by permanent vegetation cover or hard surface. The measures must\nensure that only clean stormwater leaves the site.\nReason: To ensure sediment laden runoff and site debris do not impact local stormwater\nsystems and waterways.\n40. Construction Waste and Materials\nAll construction waste must be contained on the subject land in a suitable waste enclosure\nand disposed of at a facility that may lawfully accept the waste. Building materials are to\nbe delivered directly onto the subject land. Public roads and reserves are to be maintained\nclear of rubbish, building materials and all other items.\nReason: To minimise environmental and amenity impacts of waste from the\ndevelopment.\n41. Discovery of relics and Aboriginal objects\nWhile subdivision site work is being carried out, if a person reasonably suspects a relic of\nAboriginal object is discovered:\na) the work in the area of the discovery must cease immediately.\nb) the discovery must be notified to:\ni. for a relic - the Heritage Council; or\nii. for an Aboriginal object - the person who is the authority for the\nprotection of Aboriginal objects and Aboriginal places in New South\nWales under the National Parks and Wildlife Act 1974, section 85.\nSubdivision site work may recommence at a time confirmed in writing by:\na) for a relic - the Heritage Council; or\nb) for an Aboriginal object - the person who is the authority for the protection of\nAboriginal objects and Aboriginal places in New South Wales under the National\nParks and Wildlife Act 1974, section 85.\nReason: To ensure the protection of objects of potential significance during works.\n42. Burning of Vegetation\nThe burning of vegetation felled as part of the works associated with the development is\nnot permitted.\nReason: To protect the health and amenity of surrounding occupants.\n43. Electricity Infrastructure\nSince electricity infrastructure is located within the property and/or within close proximity\nof the property, any activities approved by this consent within these locations must be\nundertaken in accordance with the latest industry guideline currently know n as ISSC 20\nGuideline for the Management of Activities within Electricity Easements and Close to\nInfrastructure.\nApproval may be required from Essential Energy should activities within the property\nencroach on the electricity infrastructure.\nReason: To minimise safety risks associated with working in proximity to power.\n44. Responsibility for changes to public infrastructure\nWhile subdivision site work is being carried out, any costs incurred as a result of the\napproved removal, relocation or reconstruction of public infrastructure (including but\nlimited to ramps, footpaths, kerb and gutter, light poles, kerb inlet pits, service provider\npits, street trees or any other infrastructure in the street footpath area) must be paid as\ndirected by the consent authority.\nReason: To ensure payment of approved changes to public infrastructure.\n45. Environmental Management Plan\nPrior to the commencement of subdivision works, a comprehensive Environmental\nManagement Plan (EMP) must be submitted to and approved by Council.\nThe EMP must include the following components as a minimum:\ng) An erosion and sediment control plan designed in accordance with the\nrequirements of the Landcom (March 2004), Managing Urban Stormwater,\nSoils and Construction which addresses:\nvi. General site management, material handling practices, soil\nstabilisation, water control, sediment control, wind erosion control\nand access measures.\nvii. Installation of erosion and sediment control measures prior to\nconstruction.\nviii. Regular inspection of erosion and sediment control measures,\nparticularly following rainfall events, to ensure their ongoing\nfunctionality.\nix. Restriction of stockpiles to identified construction compounds, in\nareas of cleared land and exotic grassland and management of\nthese stockpiles to ensure no offsite impacts through dust\ngeneration or sedimentation.\nx. Areas of bare ground to be stabilised as soon as practicable after\nconstruction to minimise the time bare earth is exposed to erosion\nand weed invasion.\nh) A vegetation management plan, including:\niii. An accurate to scale tree plan showing the trees to be removed and\nthose to be retained as part of the development.\nTrees to be retained are to be clearly identified on site by distinctive temporary taping.\nOnly the minimum extent of clearing necessary to create any APZ will be permitted. In\nthis regard, at least 14% of the tree canopy cover within the inner protection area and\n29% in the outer protection area are to be retained.\niv. Communication with construction personnel of the conservation\nvalue of surrounding habitats and their responsibilities with regards\nto protection of these habitats during construction.\ni) A weed management plan, including a description of:\nvi. Type and location of weeds of concern (including noxious weeds)\nwithin the subject site.\nvii. Sensitive receivers (such as native vegetation, drainage lines and\nwaterways) within or adjacent to the subject site.\nviii. Measures to prevent the spread of weeds, including hygiene\nprocedures for equipment, footwear and clothing.\nix. Proposed weed control methods and targeted areas.\nx. Weed disposal protocols.\nj) A fauna management plan, including, but not limited to the following:\niv. A fauna management protocol, including pre-clearing surveys for\nnesting or roosting terrestrial fauna.\nv. A habitat feature protocol, including pre-clearing surveys for habitat\nfeatures such as hollow logs and hollow-bearing trees that can be\nretained or salvaged and placed in adjoining retained vegetation,\nand protocols for the safe clearing of hollow-bearing trees to ensure\nno resident fauna are injured.\nvi. Careful removal and relocation of hollow resources cleared form the\nsubject site into proximate areas.\nk) A pre-clearing protocol which should include, but not be limited to:\nvi. Clear marking/erection of exclusion fencing around protected\nvegetation areas and delineation of 'no go' areas.\nvii. Inspections of native vegetation for other resident fauna and/or\nnests or other signs of fauna occupancy.\nviii. Capture and relocation or captive rearing of less mobile fauna (such\nas roosting microbats, nestling birds or any injured fauna) by a\ntrained and experienced wildlife handler and with assistance from\nWIRES (or similar).\nix. Inspection and identification/marking of hollow-bearing trees\nadjacent to construction footprints to help ensure against accidental\nimpacts.\nx. Pre-clearing surveys are to be undertaken by suitably qualified and\nexperienced ecologists.\nl) An A4 handout must be prepared which provides illustrations of Aboriginal\nartefacts that might be found on the subject land, a written procedure to be\nfollowed in the event that artefacts are found, and a brief warning as to the\nlikely outcome if the procedure is ignored. The handout must be provided to\nall operators of earthmoving equipment within the subject land.\nReason: To minimise the environmental impact of the development.\n46. Traffic Management Plan\nThe proponent shall prepare and implement a Traffic Management Plan endorsed by\nCouncil for the development. The plan must:\n(a) Be prepared by a suitably qualified and experienced person, in\nconsultation with Council\n(b) Detail the measures that would be implemented to ensure road\nsafety and network efficiency during development works.\n(c) Detail heavy vehicle routes, access and parking arrangements.\n(d) Include a Driver Code of Conduct to:\n(i) minimise the impacts of works on the local and regional road\nnetwork.\n(ii) minimise conflicts with other road users.\n(iii) ensure truck drivers use specified routes.\n(e) Include a program to monitor the effectiveness of these measures;\nand\n(f) If necessary, detail procedures for notifying residents and the\ncommunity (including local schools), of any potential disruptions to\nroutes.\nReason: To minimise the impacts of the development on the safe and efficient operation\nof the road network.\n47. Traffic Guidance Scheme\nThe proponent shall prepare and implement a Traffic Guidance Scheme endorsed by\nCouncil for the development. The scheme must:\n(a) Be prepared by a suitably qualified and experienced person,\n(b) Be in accordance with Traffic Control Work Sites Manual and AS1742,\nin consultation with Council.\n(c) Detail the measures that would be implemented to ensure road user\nsafety and network efficiency during works.\nReason: To minimise the impacts of the development on the safe and efficient operation\nof the road user network.\n48. Notification of Commencement\nThe proponent shall give at least two (2) days’ written notice to Council of the date work\nis intended to commence and the details of the appointed principal certifying authority\n(PCA). Where the PCA is not Council, the proponent shall also give a minimum two (2)\ndays’ notice to the PCA of the intention to commence work.\nReason: To inform the relevant authorities and facilitate auditing.\n49. Works in Road Reserve\nNo work shall occur within the road reserve until a separate consent for the work has\nbeen issued in pursuance to the Roads Act 1993. This will require a separate application\nto be made to Council for a Roads Act Consent and requires payment of an application fee\nas per Council’s Fees and Charges Schedule current at the time of application.\nReason: To ensure the legality of the development.\n50. Water Supply, Sewerage or Stormwater Drainage Work\nBefore carrying out any water supply, sewerage or stormwater drainage work, an\napproval under section 68 of the Local Government Act 1993 must be obtained for those\nactivities.\nReason: To ensure the legality of the development.\nBEFORE ISSUE OF AN SUBDIVISION CERTIFICATE\n51. Plan of Subdivision\nAn application for a Subdivision Certificate must be made on the NSW Planning Portal and\nmust include evidence demonstrating how all conditions of consent have been complied\nwith.\nReason: To comply with the provisions of the Environmental Planning & Assessment Act\n1979.\n52. Compliance report\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, the proponent must submit to Council a\nconsolidated report demonstrating compliance with all preconditions of this consent.\nReason: To ensure the development is undertaken in accordance with the consent.\n53. Section 64 Contributions\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate the developer/consent holder must be\neligible to obtain a Section 307 Certificate of Compliance under the Water Management\nAct 2000.\nThe contributions payable will be adjusted in accordance with relevant plan and the\namount payable will be calculated on the basis of the contribution rates that are\napplicable at the time of payment.\nTo be eligible, the developer/consent holder must pay the contributions (adjusted as\nrequired) set out in the following table to Council:\nPublic service No of\nEquivalent\nTenements\nContribution\nRate ($Amount\nper ET)\nContribution\nLevied\nDate until\nwhich\nContribution\nrate is\napplicable\nReticulated\nWater 4 $3700.00 $14,800.00 30 June 2025\nReason: To ensure contributions are paid in accordance with Councils Development\nServicing Plans.\n54. Section 7.12 Contributions\nPursuant to Section 7.12 of the Environmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979 , the\nmonetary contribution set out in the following table is to be paid to Council prior to the\nissue of a Subdivision Certificate.\nContribution Plan Proposed Cost of\nDevelopment\nContribution\nRate\nContribution\nLevied\nSection 7.12\nDevelopment\nContribution Plan\n$124,502.00 %1 of value of\nworks $124,5.02\nThe contributions payable will be adjusted in accordance with relevant plan and the\namount payable will be calculated on the basis of the contribution rates that are\napplicable at the time of payment.\nThe contribution is levied in accordance with the Bellingen Shire Council Section 7.12\nContributions Plan 2021, which may be viewed during office hours at Council’s\nAdministration Centre, 33 -39 Hyde Street, Bellingen or on Council’s website:\nwww.bellingen.nsw.gov.au.\nReason: To ensure contributions to the increased demand on public infrastructure arising\nfrom the development are paid in accordance with section 7.12 of the Environmental\nPlanning and Assessment Act.\n55. Compensatory Planting\nThe applicant must undertake compensatory planting for the removal of two (2) primary\nKoala Food Trees in accordance with Chapter 16 of the Bellingen Shire Development\nControl Plan 2017 in accordance with the recommendations of the approved report\ntitled Arboricultural Impact and Tree Protection Plan Report by Harbour Abor, prior to\nthe issue of the Subdivision Certificate\nThis shall include:\na) Twenty (20) juvenile Koala Food Trees comprising the following species:\n• Eucalyptus microcorys (10)\n• Eucalyptus propinqua (5)\n• Eucalyptus saligna (5)\nb) All replacement trees must:\n• Be sourced from local provenance seed stock\n• Be planted in ecologically appropriate areas within or adjacent to the mapped\nSecondary “A” Koala Habitat Area on the site\n• Be protected using suitable tree guards to prevent browsing damage\n• Be planted and maintained in accordance with the arborist’s recommendations\nin Section 6.2 of the Arboricultural Impact Report\nc) All compensatory planting must be completed prior to the issue of a Subdivision\nCertificate, or within a timeframe agreed in writing by Council.\nd) A qualified ecologist or bush regenerator must be engaged to identify suitable\nplanting locations.\ne) A report confirming completion of the planting and listing species, provenance,\nquantities must be submitted to Council within 14 days of planting.\nReason: To ensure the impact on Koala feed trees and native vegetation removal is\nmitigated.\n56. Community Management Statement\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate a final Community Management Statement\nis to be submitted to and approved by Council’s General Manager or delegate. The\nstatement is to include provisions for but not limited to the use of the access road by\nemergency services, bona fide members of the public and public authorities. The\nstatement must also include a Utility Servicing Plan showing works as executed (WAE)\nReason: To ensure the legislation.\n57. Maintenance of Common Property\nThe Community Title Subdivision Plan and Community Title Management Statement is\nto ensure, stormwater systems basins, replacement trees, and access that are to be\ncontained within common property that remain the responsibility of the body corporate\nto maintain the following performance criteria:\na) All plants should be retained and protected. Plants requiring replacement must\nbe same species or fulfill a similar role and be locally occurring Australian Native\nSpecies, and any conditions of this consent relevant to compensatory planting;\nb) All trees must be left to grow with a natural shape without lopping of it canopy\nor roots. Any pruning must be in accordance with Australian Standard AS4373-\n2007 Pruning of Amenity Trees\nc) 95% control of environmental weed species\nd) No areas of weed incursion greater than 10m2\nReason: To ensure the maintenance of subdivision common property.\n58. Plan of Subdivision and Section 88B Instrument requirements\nA Section 88B Instrument is to be submitted with the application for a Subdivision\nCertificate. The final plan of subdivision and accompanying Section 88B Instrument are\nto provide for the items in the following list:\nDwelling Envelope - The indicative dwelling envelopes from the approved plan shall be\nshown on the plan of subdivision. A restriction on the use of land shall be created to\nlimit the erection of dwellings to the indicative dwelling envelopes, unless site\ninvestigation reports demonstrating the suitability of any alternate sites, to the\nsatisfaction of Council, are obtained prior to development consent or a complying\ndevelopment certificate being granted for a dwelling.\nEasement for Services - The creation of suitable easements for services for proposed\nLot(s).\nEasement for Electricity - The creation of any necessary easements for electricity\npurposes as required by the electricity supply authority.\nOn-site Sewage Management Systems - Restriction as to user requiring the\nimplementation of the approved report “Land Capability Assessment Report- 70 Henry\nBoultwood, Fernmount Ref - 2021-54-02 A” by Earth Water dated 13/02/2025 on each\nlot containing a dwelling envelope.\n[Reason: To ensure the final plan of subdivision is consistent with this development\nconsent.\n59. Quality Assurance Documentation\nPrior to the Issue of a subdivision Certificate, the proponent shall submit and have\napproved all quality testing required for compliance with ‘Standard Conditions for\nEngineering Works Associated with Developments’ and the conditions of this consent.\nReason: To ensure Council’s records accurately reflect development undertaken in the\nShire.\n60. Certificate of Compliance - Water Management Act 2000\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, the proponent shall obtain a certificate of\ncompliance under section 307 of the Water Management Act 2000 evidencing that\nadequate arrangements have been made for the provision of water services to and\nwithin the development.\nNote: Section 64 of the Local Government Act 1993 authorises Council to issue\ncertificates of compliance under section 306 of the Water Management Act 2000.\nSection 64 of the Local Government Act 1993 also authorises Council to impose pre-\nconditions to the issuing of certificates of compliance.\nAs a precondition to the issuing of a certificate of compliance, Council requires the\npayment of developer charges for the cost of works to augment the water supply\nsystem and sewer mains, pumping stations and treatment works as prescribed by\nCouncil's adopted section 64 plans.\nA developer charges notice of payment is attached to this consent and outlines\nmonetary contributions and unit rates applicable at the time of issue of this consent.\nReason: To provide adequate services; contribution towards services and facilities.\n61. Certificate of Compliance (Engineering)\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, a compliance certificate prepared by a\nsuitably qualified and practicing Civil Engineer (that being either an engineer eligible for\nadmission as either a Chartered Professional Engineer CPEng or a Registered Professional\nEngineer, RPEng) shall be submitted to Council.\nThe compliance certificate shall certify construction of the required Council infrastructure\nworks in accordance with the approved design and specifications included in this\ndevelopment consent and associated authorisations under the Roads Act 1993,\nEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979 and Local Government Act 1993.\nReason: The likely impacts of the development in respect of traffic, utilities, drainage and\npublic interest.\n62. Certificates for Engineering Works\nPrior to the issue of any Subdivision Certificate, the submission to Council of all test\ncertificates, owner’s manuals, warranties and operating instructions for civil works,\nmechanical and/or electrical plant, together with a certificate from a suitably qualified\nengineer is required certifying that all works have been constructed in accordance with\nthe approved plans and Council’s Adopted Engineering Standard.\nReason: To ensure Council has detailed information on assets to be transferred to\nCouncil.\n63. Certificate for Pipes, Access Driveways, etc Within Easements\nPrior to the issue of any Subdivision Certificate, a certificate from a Registered Surveyor\nis to be submitted to Council certifying that all pipelines, structures, access driveways\nand/or services are located wholly within the relevant easements.\nReason: To ensure services are within proposed easements.\n64. Water Connection\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, separate water supply service connections\nmust be provided for each proposed lot.\nNote: Reticulated water connection up to the metre position at the public road frontage\nis provided by Council upon payment in accordance with the adopted Schedule of Fees\nand Charges.\nReason: To ensure the provision of essential utility infrastructure.\n65. Water connection points for new lots\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate, all lots shall be provided with a separate\nreticulated water connection and meter at no cost to Council. Water meters shall be\npositioned at the public road frontage of the lot. Any connections from the water main to\nthe meter shall be carried out by Council.\nNote: The standard rates in Council’s Schedule of Fees and Charges current at the time of\napplication for reticulated water connections will apply.\nReason: The likely impacts of the development in respect of utilities.\n66. New water, stormwater and road assets\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate all stormwater, reticulated water and\nroadworks detailed in the approved plans shall be constructed and approved by Council.\nReason: To ensure the development does not cause adverse environmental impacts.\n67. Civil Works Construction\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, the relevant civil works including driveway\nworks, works approved under the Road Act Consent, stormwater drainage works, sewer\nconnections and water connections detailed in the endorsed plans shall be constructed\nand deemed completed by the principle certifying authority.\nReason: The likely impacts of the development in respect of construction, traffic and\nsafety.\n68. Stormwater Management Plan\nPrior to release of any Subdivision Certificate the applicant shall submit and have\napproved a site -based stormwater device maintenance and management plan detailing\nthe maintenance requirements, maintenance intervals and other such relevant\ninformation for any new stormwater quality treatment device asset handed to Council.\nReason: To provide Council maintenance staff with specific maintenance advice for the\ncontrols implemented on the site.\n69. Batter Stability\nAny batters steeper than 1(v) in 2(h) and greater than 2m in height will require assessment\nby a geotechnical engineering verifying the stability of the batter slope.\nReason: To ensure the future stability of earthworks.\n70. Repair of Damaged Infrastructure\nPrior to the issue of a Subdivision Certificate the proponent shall repair any public\ninfrastructure deemed damaged because of the works.\nReason: To ensure that works do not degrade public infrastructure.\n71. Bush Fire Safety Authority - Certificate of compliance\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, certification from a suitably qualified person\nthat the conditions of the Bush Fire Safety Authority have been fulfilled shall be submitted\nto Council.\nReason: To ensure compliance with the bush fire safety authority.\n72. Electricity supply\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, Council must be supplied with a Notice of\nArrangement from Essential Energy or an authorised provider stating that satisfactory\narrangements have been made for the provision of reticulated electricity to eac h\nproposed lot, and that easements for electricity purposes satisfactory to Essential Energy\nare supplied on the plan of subdivision over existing and proposed electricity lines\npursuant to section 88B of the Conveyancing Act 1919.\nReason: To ensure the provision of electricity supply.\n73. Underground electricity - Community title subdivision\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate:\n(a) each proposed community title lot shall be physically provisioned with\nreticulated underground electrical power; and\n(b) Council must be supplied with a Notice of Arrangement from Essential\nEnergy or an authorised provider stating that satisfactory arrangements\nhave been made for the provision of underground reticulated electricity to\nthe subdivision.\nNote 1: All electrical work shall comply with AS3000, NSW Service and Installation Rules\nand where applicable Essential Energy Design and Construction Standards.\nNote 2: Any new customer connection to the electricity network will require an\napplication to be lodged through the NECF portal on the Essential Energy homepage.\nReason: To ensure the provision of electricity supply.\n74. Telecommunications\nUnderground telephone services shall be provided to each proposed lot to the\nsatisfaction of a telecommunications carrier licensed under the Telecommunications Act\n1997 (Cth).\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, Council shall be supplied with:\na) certification from a suitably qualified electrical engineer that the works specified\nhave been installed and evidence that a telecommunications carrier licensed under\nthe Telecommunications Act 1997 has agreed to take ownership of the\ninfrastructure; or\nb) a certificate of practical completion from NBN Co; or\nc) a ‘Telecommunications Agreement Advice Letter’ from a telecommunications carrier\nlicenced under the Telecommunications Act 1997 confirming that the\ntelecommunications carrier has been engaged to install telecommunications\ninfrastructure to the proposed development; or\nd) a ‘Network Infrastructure Provisioning Confirmation Letter’ from a\ntelecommunications carrier licenced under the Telecommunications Act 1997\nconfirming that telecommunications infrastructure has been installed to the\nproposed development.\nReason: To ensure the provision of communication services.\n75. Landscaping\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, a works as executed plan shall be submitted\nto the Principal Certifier certifying that all landscape works have been carried out in\naccordance with the approved plan.\nReason: To preserve and encourage landscaped settings.\n76. Completion of All Works\nPrior to the issue of any Subdivision Certificate, all subdivision works required by this\ndevelopment consent and associated subdivision works certificate must be completed.\nIn the event lots are released in stages, only works relating to those lots need be\ncompleted to the satisfaction of Council.\nReason: To ensure all works are completed prior to registration of the subdivision plan.\n77. Completion of Access\nThe vehicle access works shall be constructed and maintained in accordance with the\nrequired Road Act Consent Approval in pursuance to the Roads Act 1993.\nReason: To ensure that vehicular access to and egress from the development site is\ndesigned and constructed to Council’s standards.\n78. Works as Executed Plans\nPrior to the issue of a subdivision certificate, the proponent shall submit and have\napproved works as executed plans (completion drawings) in accordance with Standard\nConditions for Engineering Works Associated with Developments and Water Services\nAssociation of Australia for Council infrastructure works.\nReason: To ensure Council’s records accurately reflect development undertaken in the\nShire.\n79. Final Subdivision Plan Requirements\nA final Subdivision Plan shall be prepared by a Registered Surveyor and include (but not\nbe limited to) the following:\n(a) Required Easements\nThe following easements shall be created by Section 88B of the Conveyancing Act 1919\non the Final Subdivision Plan.\ni. Any necessary Easements to Drain Water, (minimum 1m wide).\nii. Any necessary Easements for Services (minimum 300mm wide).\nReason: To ensure the required easements and restrictions are completed before the land\nis subdivided.\nGeneral Terms of Approval\nNSW RURAL FIRE SERVICE","stage":"general","stage_basis":"heading","page":8}]});
